<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Landgraviate of Hesse</h1><p class="st-meta">1264 CE – 1567 CE · capital Marburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Central German landgraviate that broke away from Thuringia in 1264 and, under Philip the Magnanimous, became a leading Protestant power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the Ludowingian landgraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> died out in 1247, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophie-of-brabant">Sophie of Brabant</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettins</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-thuringian-succession">War of the Thuringian Succession</a> to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> for her son Henry I the Child. The settlement of 1264 separated Hesse from Thuringia, and in 1292 Henry was made an imperial prince.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The landgraves ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, expanding by acquiring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-katzenelnbogen">County of Katzenelnbogen</a> in 1479. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-landgrave-of-hesse">Philip I the Magnanimous</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1526, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-marburg">University of Marburg</a> in 1527, hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy">Marburg Colloquy</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Luther</a> and Zwingli in 1529 and co-led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>. His bigamous second marriage weakened the Protestant cause.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Philip's death in 1567, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> was divided among his four sons into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hesse">Hesse-Kassel</a>, Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>, Hesse-Rheinfels and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Hesse-Darmstadt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> became the main successors, later the Electorate of Hesse and the Grand Duchy of Hesse.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/landgraviate-of-hesse.jpg" alt="Landgraviate of Hesse" width="320" height="305"><figcaption>Image: Heiliges_Römisches_Reich_1400.png: Gustav Droysen derivative work: ++gardenfriend++ (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gft._Solms_Lgft._Hessen_Gft._Ziegenhain.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1264 CE – 1567 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Marburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry I the Child, Louis I of Hesse, Philip I the Magnanimous</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hessian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hessen</dd></dl></aside></div>
